        { "k": "C", "text": "An Obsidian note in SecondBrain, regenerated by script, with links to every project." },
        { "k": "D", "text": "The localhost:7373 command centre, with a projects tab." }
      ],
      "rec": { "option": "A", "why": "Your work starts in a Claude Code session on both machines, and the parts that change state (slate-check, seven-day --apply, mark-done) are already CLI verbs. One command that both shows and acts keeps reading and changing in the same place, so the record stays current. The board and command centre become read-only renders of the same data. The trade-off: no clickable visual front door. The board stays for scanning all 171 rows, but you don't manage from it. B looks better but needs a write-back path from the browser, and that's where the board work stalled in August." },
      "status": "open", "durable": true, "updated": false, "thread": []
    },
    {
      "id": "q3", "round": 1, "deps": [],
      "title": "Where do tasks inside a project live?",
      "body": "A registry row has one `next_action` string. Each repo already has docs/STATUS.md with a `## Next` list, which syncs across machines through git, and crosswalk already flags active/paused repos whose `## Next` is empty. 22 Drive-only projects have no repo and no STATUS.md. What hangs on this: whether the PM system owns tasks or only points at them.",
      "options": [
        { "k": "A", "text": "In each repo's STATUS.md `## Next` (the PM system reads it, never writes tasks itself). Drive-only projects keep tasks in the registry row." },
